{
  "decision": "REVISE",
  "detail": {
    "days": 14,
    "checkpoint": "A first working Python expenses report that reads a small CSV into rows, groups amounts by category with a correct total per category, flags malformed rows with a stated reason instead of silently dropping them, and then reproduces this correctly on one changed CSV you supply."
  },
  "plans": [
    {
      "planRef": "plan_1",
      "blueprint": {
        "schemaVersion": 2,
        "strategy": {
          "policy": "ROADMAP",
          "outcome": "Use my uploaded course note to build a small CSV expenses-report project. Verify quotations and course applicability; it is my development course note, not an official public syllabus.",
          "explanation": "The note is a private development course note with no verified external syllabus, weighting or assessment structure, so nothing can be credited from prior work and the whole outcome is a forward project. The useful next checkpoint is a small but complete first version of the report covering all three requested scope areas: reading CSV rows, grouping totals by category, and detecting and explaining malformed rows. These three are tightly coupled in one artifact, so one integrated build with a short planning step is more honest than splitting them into separate activities. The changed-data example is folded into the same build as the final independent demonstration rather than a separate phase. Later engineering, tooling and further milestones stay strategic and unbooked until this checkpoint works and is independently demonstrated.",
          "recommendedEffort": {
            "lowMinutes": 45,
            "likelyMinutes": 90,
            "highMinutes": 150
          },
          "phases": [
            {
              "localId": "phase_build",
              "title": "First working expenses report",
              "outcome": "A running Python script that reads a small CSV into rows, groups amounts by category with correct totals, flags malformed rows with a stated reason, and is demonstrated correctly on one changed CSV.",
              "effort": {
                "lowMinutes": 45,
                "likelyMinutes": 90,
                "highMinutes": 150
              },
              "readinessCriterion": "The script runs on the original CSV and on one changed CSV, produces a correct category-to-total mapping for both, lists any malformed rows with a reason, and you can explain each part without assistance."
            }
          ]
        },
        "scope": [
          {
            "scopeRef": "scope_1",
            "disposition": "INCLUDED",
            "contribution": "Reading CSV rows is the input stage of the report and is exercised in the integrated build, including a quoted-comma field so parsing is correct rather than naive splitting."
          },
          {
            "scopeRef": "scope_2",
            "disposition": "INCLUDED",
            "contribution": "Grouping amounts by category and reporting each total is the core computation of the report and is exercised in the integrated build."
          },
          {
            "scopeRef": "scope_3",
            "disposition": "INCLUDED",
            "contribution": "Detecting and explaining malformed rows rather than silently excluding them is exercised in the integrated build, with reasons reported for each flagged row."
          }
        ],
        "activities": [
          {
            "localId": "build_report",
            "outcomeRefs": [
              "phase_build"
            ],
            "scopeRefs": [
              "scope_1",
              "scope_2",
              "scope_3"
            ],
            "title": "Build and demonstrate the first expenses report",
            "activityType": "Integrated project build",
            "purpose": "Produce one small working report that reads CSV rows, groups totals by category, flags malformed rows with reasons, and is then demonstrated on a changed CSV.",
            "instructions": "Use only the Python course material you have actually been taught; do not reach for libraries or methods outside that taught repertoire. Work in a single Python file named expenses_report.py.\n\n1. Create a small CSV file expenses.csv with a header row and about six data rows. Columns: date, category, amount. Use at least one quoted field containing a comma (for example a category written as \"food, dining\") so you test real CSV parsing rather than splitting on commas by hand. Include amounts as plain numbers.\n\n2. Write expenses_report.py so it:\n   - reads expenses.csv into rows using a CSV reader appropriate to your course material;\n   - skips the header row correctly;\n   - groups amounts by category and prints each category with its total;\n   - detects malformed rows. Treat a row as malformed when, for example, it does not have the expected number of fields, or its amount is not a valid number. For each malformed row, print the row and a short reason (such as 'wrong number of fields' or 'amount is not a number') instead of silently dropping it.\n\n3. Run the script on expenses.csv. Check that every category total matches a total you compute by hand, and that each malformed row you planted is reported with a reason.\n\n4. Then create a second file expenses_changed.csv with different categories, different amounts and at least one different malformed row, and run the script on it. Check the new totals by hand and confirm the new malformed row is reported with a reason.\n\n5. Be ready to explain, in your own words, how each of the three parts works: reading rows, grouping totals, and detecting malformed rows. If any part fails on either file, fix it and re-run before considering the checkpoint done.\n\nKeep the two CSV files and the script; they are the evidence for the checkpoint.",
            "completionCriterion": "expenses_report.py runs without error on both expenses.csv and expenses_changed.csv; for each file it prints a category-to-total mapping that matches hand-computed totals and reports each malformed row with a stated reason; you can explain the reading, grouping and malformed-row detection steps without assistance.",
            "effort": {
              "lowMinutes": 45,
              "likelyMinutes": 90,
              "highMinutes": 150
            },
            "timeboxMinutes": 90,
            "commitment": {
              "category": "COMMITTED"
            },
            "hardDependencies": [],
            "splitPoints": [
              {
                "afterMinutes": 40,
                "completionCriterion": "expenses.csv exists with a quoted-comma field and a planted malformed row, and expenses_report.py reads it into rows and prints the rows correctly."
              }
            ],
            "stoppingCondition": "Stop when both CSV files produce correct category totals and report every malformed row with a reason, and you can explain the three parts; do not add extra features, libraries or a second report in this checkpoint."
          }
        ],
        "decisions": {
          "assumptions": [],
          "tradeOffs": [
            "The three scope areas are built together in one integrated activity rather than as separate read/group/error activities, because they are tightly coupled in a single small artifact and separating them would add coordination without adding evidence.",
            "The changed-data demonstration is folded into the same build as its final step rather than a separate activity, because the course note requires it as part of a successful report, not as an additional deliverable.",
            "Later engineering, tooling and further project milestones are left strategic and unbooked; they will need fresh evidence and an explicit request before being detailed."
          ],
          "unresolvedUncertainty": [
            "The course note is a private development note with no verified external syllabus, weighting or assessment structure, so no prior work can be credited and no formal assessment alignment is claimed.",
            "No public-source research was available, so the note's authority and completeness could not be independently checked."
          ],
          "stoppingConditions": [
            "Stop the plan when the checkpoint is met: the script runs correctly on the original and the changed CSV, totals match hand computation, malformed rows are reported with reasons, and you can explain the three parts.",
            "Do not start further project milestones or tooling within this plan until the checkpoint is independently demonstrated and you explicitly request expansion."
          ]
        }
      }
    }
  ],
  "sharedDependencies": [],
  "tradeOffExplanation": "The plan spends its effort on one integrated build rather than three separate activities, because reading rows, grouping totals and detecting malformed rows are tightly coupled in a single small report and separating them would add coordination without adding evidence. The changed-data example is the final step of that build, not a separate phase, matching the note's requirement that a successful report includes it. Nothing is credited because the note is a private development course note with no verified external assessment structure, and no prior execution exists. Later engineering, tooling and further milestones remain strategic and unbooked until the checkpoint is demonstrated and you explicitly ask to expand."
}

